            '# Prefix Sum',
            '',
            '> **Definition.** A prefix-sum array `pref` stores the running total of the input: `pref[0] = 0` and `pref[i] = a[0] + a[1] + ... + a[i-1]`. With it, **any contiguous subarray sum is answered in O(1)** — you build once in O(n), then each query is a single subtraction.',
            '',
            '## History & Origin',
            '',
            'Cumulative totals are one of the oldest arithmetic ideas — tally boards and abacus methods keep running sums while adding in sequence. As a named algorithmic technique the prefix sum (also called **cumulative sum**, or **scan**) became prominent when Hillis and Steele showed in a 1986 CACM paper that all prefixes can be computed in parallel in O(log n) on many processors, making scan a primitive of parallel programming. [Wikipedia: Prefix sum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prefix_sum), [Hillis & Steele 1986 (CACM)](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/12130.12132)',
            '',
            'For everyday single-threaded code the idea is even simpler: replace a repeated O(n) re-scan with one precomputed O(n) pass, then answer every range query with `pref[r+1] - pref[l]`.',
