            '## Why It Works (proof)',
            '',
            'Let `dp[i]` be the **maximum sum of any subarray ending exactly at index `i`**. A subarray ending at `i` either has length 1 (just `a[i]`) or extends some subarray ending at `i-1`. Therefore:',
            '',
            '> **`dp[i] = max(a[i], dp[i-1] + a[i])`**',
            '',
            'This is correct by induction: the only subarrays ending at `i` are the single element or an extension of a subarray ending at `i-1`, and among the latter the best one has sum `dp[i-1]`. The global answer is `max(dp[0..n-1])`, because every non-empty subarray has a unique last index. [GeeksforGeeks: Kadane\'s Algorithm](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/largest-sum-contiguous-subarray/)',
            '',
            'Since `dp[i]` depends only on `dp[i-1]`, we keep just two variables (`cur` and `best`) — no array needed. The `cur < 0` branch is the same recurrence in disguise: when `dp[i-1] < 0`, `max(a[i], dp[i-1] + a[i])` is always `a[i]`, so restarting is safe.',
            '',
            '## Complexity',
            '',
            '| Aspect | Value | Why |',
