            '| **Maximum product** | Track both max and min — signs flip on negatives | O(n) |',
            '| **2D (max rectangle)** | Compress rows into prefix sums, run Kadane per column band | O(min(r,c)²·max(r,c)) |',
            '| **Recover the subarray** | Record `l` when restarting, `r` when `best` updates | O(n) |',
            '| **Max length at least k** | Kadane with a min-prefix kept in a sliding window | O(n) |',
            '',
            '## Edge Cases',
            '',
            '1. **All-negative array** (`[-5, -2, -9]`) → the algorithm returns the **least negative** element (`-2`). The restart rule guarantees the answer is never empty. (Note: the original algorithm Kadane described gives `0` for this case — see the History section.)',
            '2. **Single element** (`[7]`) → returns `7`; the loop never runs and `best` keeps its seed.',
            '3. **Empty input** → `a[0]` would crash. Guard for emptiness first (decide whether an empty subarray is allowed).',
            '4. **Very large sums** → use a 64-bit integer (`long`/`long long`) because `cur + a[i]` can overflow 32 bits.',
            '5. **All zeros / positives** (`[0, 0]`, `[2, 3]`) → sum grows monotonically; `best` is simply the total.',
            '',
            '## Common Mistakes',
            '',
            '- **Seeding `best` with 0 instead of `a[0]`** — silently breaks all-negative arrays.',
            '- **Forgetting to update `best`** — you get only the best subarray *ending at the last index*, not the global best.',
            '- **Looping from 0 instead of 1** — processes the first element twice.',
            '- **Confusing subarray (contiguous) with subsequence (any order)** — Kadane does not solve the subsequence problem (that is just the sum of all positives).',
            '- **Writing `cur + a[i]` without the restart** — the classic bug that makes negative prefixes poison the whole result.',
            '',
            '## Applications',
            '',
            'Kadane\'s algorithm appears everywhere a "best contiguous stretch" is needed:',
            '',
            '- **Image & astronomy analysis** — the original motivation: locating regions of high response in digitized pictures and radio/optical data. [MDPI retrospective](https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4893/16/11/519)',
            '- **Genomics / bioinformatics** — finding the highest-scoring segment of a DNA sequence by scoring matches and mismatches. [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximum_subarray_problem)',
            '- **Stock trading** — convert prices to daily changes; the maximum subarray is the best buy-sell window.',
            '- **ML & signal processing** — a routine subroutine inside 2D maximum-sum-rectangle problems. [Bentley 1984](https://www.cs.rpi.edu/~moorthy/Courses/CSCI2300/p865-bentley.pdf)',
            '',
            '> [!tip] Try it yourself',
            '> Solve **LeetCode 53 (Maximum Subarray)** and **GFG "Largest Sum Contiguous Subarray"** by hand on the trace above first — then compare with the five programs on this page.'
