            '# Hashing & Frequency Arrays',
            '',
            '> **Definition.** Hashing trades space for time: store seen values or counts in a table keyed by the value itself, turning O(n) lookups into **O(1) amortized**. It is the go-to for "has it appeared before?", "how many times?", and "does the complement exist?".',
            '',
            '## History & Origin',
            '',
            'The idea of hashing is due to **Hans Peter Luhn**, an IBM engineer, in an internal 1953 IBM memorandum — the same man who invented the KWIC (keyword-in-context) indexing method and the Luhn checksum still used to validate credit-card numbers. Open addressing was formalised by W. W. Peterson in 1957, and Donald Knuth analysed hash functions in *The Art of Computer Programming*. [Wikipedia: Hash table](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash_table), [Wikipedia: Hans Peter Luhn](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Peter_Luhn)',
            '',
            'For coding interviews the exact collision strategy matters little: you use the language\'s hash map (or a plain array when keys are small bounded integers) and reason about O(1) average lookups.',
            '',
