            '# Merge Intervals',
            '',
            '> **Definition.** An interval is a range `[start, end]`. Two intervals **overlap** when `a.start <= b.end && b.start <= a.end` (touching at a point counts, unless the problem says otherwise). Merging replaces every maximal chain of overlapping intervals with a single interval spanning the whole chain.',
            '',
            '## History & Origin',
            '',
            'Merging intervals is the textbook **sweep-line** algorithm: sort by one coordinate, then walk the line once. The plane-sweep paradigm became a cornerstone of computational geometry in the 1980s, and the interview version is LeetCode 56 "Merge Intervals". [LeetCode 56: Merge Intervals](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-intervals/) · [Wikipedia: Sweep line algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweep_line_algorithm)',
            '',
            '## Intuition',
            '',
            'Once the intervals are sorted by start, any overlap is **local**: interval `i` can only overlap the running interval that ends latest among everything seen so far. So you never look back — a single pass over the sorted list is enough.',
