            '## Why It Works (invariant)',
            '',
            'The predicate `feasible(x)` is **monotone**: if a cap of `x` fits, any larger cap also fits (allowing more per part can never force more parts). So as `x` increases, `feasible` flips from false to true exactly once. The loop maintains the invariant "the answer lies in `[lo, hi]`": every `hi = mid` keeps a feasible cap in range, and every `lo = mid + 1` discards only infeasible caps. When `lo == hi`, the single remaining candidate is the smallest feasible cap, which is precisely the minimized largest sum.',
